<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Entitlements for the hardened runtime of "Bee Memory Bank.app" (used only by the optional signing branch of
  scripts/pack-macos-full.sh and by scripts/validate-macos-full-adhoc.sh; an unsigned build ignores this file).

  The .NET runtime compiles code at run time, which the hardened runtime forbids unless it is allowed explicitly:
    allow-jit                        the JIT compiler (CoreCLR)
    allow-unsigned-executable-memory executable memory the runtime writes itself (stubs, tiered compilation, ONNX Runtime)
  They are given to EVERY executable of the bundle that hosts the runtime, not only to the main one: the desktop shell
  (BeeMemoryBank.Desktop), bmbd, the API, the web host, the bmb command-line tool and the bundled "dotnet" muxer are separate
  processes, each with its own JIT. Libraries (*.dylib) carry no entitlements of their own.
  The app is NOT sandboxed on purpose: it starts child processes, opens vault folders anywhere on disk, listens on local ports,
  and keeps its keys in the user's Keychain; none of that works inside the App Sandbox.
  Every Mach-O file in the bundle (the runtime's too) is re-signed with the same identity as the app, so library validation can
  stay ON. Add com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation here only after a verified failure proves that a dependency cannot
  be re-signed, and write down which one and why.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
  <true/>
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory</key>
  <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
